import AutoAnimate from "@formkit/auto-animate/marko"

static const SNIPPET = `<AutoAnimate parent=fastRef options={ duration: 150, easing: "ease-in" }/>
<AutoAnimate parent=slowRef options={ duration: 1500, easing: "ease-in-out" }/>`;

static const START = [
  { id: 1, text: "Row 1" },
  { id: 2, text: "Row 2" },
  { id: 3, text: "Row 3" },
  { id: 4, text: "Row 4" },
  { id: 5, text: "Row 5" },
];

<let/fastItems = [...START]>
<let/slowItems = [...START]>
<let/nextId = 6>

<h2>Options: duration &amp; easing</h2>
<p class="lede">
  The <code>options</code> attribute takes the same object as the core:
  <code>duration</code> (ms) and <code>easing</code>. Both lists receive the
  same mutation at the same instant — the left snaps in 150ms, the right
  glides for 1500ms.
</p>

<div class="toolbar">
  <button onClick() {
    const row = { id: nextId, text: `Row ${nextId}` };
    fastItems = [row, ...fastItems];
    slowItems = [row, ...slowItems];
    nextId++;
  }>Add to top of both</button>
  <button onClick() {
    fastItems = fastItems.slice(1);
    slowItems = slowItems.slice(1);
  }>Remove first from both</button>
  <button onClick() {
    fastItems = [...fastItems].reverse();
    slowItems = [...slowItems].reverse();
  }>Reverse both</button>
</div>

<div class="compare">
  <div>
    <h3>150ms, ease-in</h3>
    <ul/fastRef class="demo-list">
      <for|item| of=fastItems by="id">
        <li>${item.text}</li>
      </for>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h3>1500ms, ease-in-out</h3>
    <ul/slowRef class="demo-list">
      <for|item| of=slowItems by="id">
        <li>${item.text}</li>
      </for>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>
<AutoAnimate parent=fastRef options={ duration: 150, easing: "ease-in" }/>
<AutoAnimate parent=slowRef options={ duration: 1500, easing: "ease-in-out" }/>

<pre class="snippet"><code>${SNIPPET}</code></pre>

<p class="hint">
  Why does removing feel slower than adding at the same duration? It's the
  library's default effects, not the timer. A removed row fades out visibly
  for the full duration while the rows below slide up — lots to watch. An
  entering row never moves: it's held invisible for the first half of its
  animation, then simply fades in, so it reads as an instant "pop" even
  though it runs just as long. The visible slide on add comes from the
  existing rows making room, which is why this demo inserts at the top. (See
  the plugin page for a custom add effect that is clearly visible.)
  auto-animate also respects the OS "reduce motion" setting by default; the
  core exposes <code>disrespectUserMotionPreference</code> to override that,
  but using it is not recommended.
</p>
